{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts sit ~3–10 EUR/t above realized prices across most horizons; apply a downward level shift (larger when recent trend is down).",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: after a local peak and subsequent selloff, the model declines too slowly and keeps predicting rebounds that do not materialize; increase sensitivity to negative momentum (steepen projected slope down).",
    "False mean-reversion: model repeatedly projects a return toward mid/high-70s while outcomes continue trending lower; suppress mean-reversion signals when price is below recent 10–20d average and that average is falling.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: overestimation widens with forecast horizon (especially days ~10–30); use a horizon-scaled downward adjustment (e.g., small cut near-term, larger cut longer-term).",
    "Overshoot on rebound days: when the model does call an uptick, it tends to overshoot the magnitude and level; cap rebound amplitude relative to recent realized volatility and keep rebounds below the falling moving average in bearish regimes."
  ]
}
